---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PlatformIcon from "../../components/PlatformIcon.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Requirements", href: "#requirements" },
  { label: "Desktop app", href: "#desktop" },
  { label: "Providers", href: "#providers" },
  { label: "Mobile", href: "#mobile" },
  { label: "From source", href: "#source" },
  { label: "Next steps", href: "#next" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Install"
  description="Get the Modesto desktop app on the machine where your agents will work, then install and authenticate at least one provider CLI."
  currentPath="/docs/install"
  eyebrow="Start here"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="requirements">
    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <p>
      Modesto is a local-first control plane. The agents run on the machine that
      hosts the app — not in a Modesto cloud. Set that machine up first.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Desktop builds for
        <span class="platform-inline"><PlatformIcon platform="macos" />macOS</span>,
        <span class="platform-inline"><PlatformIcon platform="windows" />Windows</span>,
        and
        <span class="platform-inline"><PlatformIcon platform="linux" />Linux</span>.
        macOS builds are Developer ID signed and notarized.
      </li>
      <li>At least one coding-agent CLI, installed and signed in on that same machine.</li>
      <li>
        From-source development additionally needs Bun 1.3.9+ and Node.js 24.13.1+.
        The shipped desktop app includes its own runtime.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>You can open Modesto before any provider is ready.</strong>
      <p>
        Provider login is required before you start a thread with that agent, not
        before you launch the app. A provider that is not authenticated shows its
        status in settings and fails at session start with the login command to run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="desktop">
    <h2>Desktop app</h2>
    <p>
      Download the latest build from the{" "}
      <a href="/download">download page</a> or{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/Syphon1205/Modesto/releases">GitHub Releases</a>.
      Installed copies update themselves through the in-app updater — you only
      download once. See <a href="/docs/updating">Updating</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-card-grid">
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span class="platform-kicker"><PlatformIcon platform="macos" />macOS</span>
        <h3>Apple silicon or Intel</h3>
        <p>Use the matching signed <code>.dmg</code>. Apple silicon is <code>arm64</code>; Intel is <code>x64</code>.</p>
        <a href="/download">Download →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span class="platform-kicker"><PlatformIcon platform="windows" />Windows</span>
        <h3>Windows 10 and 11</h3>
        <p>Use the x64 installer. WSL2 can host Linux-backed projects and provider CLIs.</p>
        <a href="/download">Download →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span class="platform-kicker"><PlatformIcon platform="linux" />Linux</span>
        <h3>x86_64 AppImage</h3>
        <p>Make the AppImage executable, then launch it. Keep the file where you intend to run it from.</p>
        <a href="/download">Download →</a>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p>
      Modesto stores local state under <code>~/.modesto</code> by default.
      <code>MODESTO_HOME</code> overrides that path. Existing profiles are copied
      once when Modesto has no state of its own — legacy data is never deleted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="providers">
    <h2>Install a provider CLI</h2>
    <p>
      Modesto drives provider CLIs; it does not ship them. Install the CLI for
      each agent you want, then authenticate it on the same machine that runs
      Modesto. Open <strong>Settings → Providers</strong> to enable an instance
      and, if needed, set an explicit binary path.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div>
        <strong>Claude Code</strong>
        <span>Install the Claude Code CLI, then run <code>claude auth login</code>.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Codex</strong>
        <span>Install the Codex CLI, then run <code>codex login</code>.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Cursor</strong>
        <span>
          Install Cursor CLI. The binary Modesto looks for is <code>cursor-agent</code>;
          the login command is <code>agent login</code>.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Gemini</strong>
        <span>Install the Gemini CLI and complete its native login.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Grok</strong>
        <span>Install the Grok CLI, then run <code>grok login</code>.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Meta (Muse Code)</strong>
        <span>
          Install the Muse CLI (<code>muse</code>), then run{" "}
          <code>muse login</code> or set <code>META_API_KEY</code>. Sessions
          use <code>muse --acp</code>. Enabled by default.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>GitHub Copilot</strong>
        <span>
          Install the Copilot CLI (<code>copilot</code>), enable the instance
          in settings, then complete Copilot’s native login.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>OpenCode</strong>
        <span>Install OpenCode, then run <code>opencode auth login</code>.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Others</strong>
        <span>
          Factory Droid, Kilo, Pi, Antigravity, Devin, Poolside, Kimi, and Qwen
          follow the same pattern: install their CLI, sign in, confirm Modesto can
          discover the binary. See <a href="/docs/providers">Providers</a>.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Each CLI must be on the app’s <code>PATH</code>, or have an explicit
      <strong>Binary path</strong> in the provider instance. Use the explicit path
      when a version manager or a non-standard install keeps the CLI off the PATH
      of the process that started Modesto.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile">
    <h2>Phone and another computer</h2>
    <p>
      The desktop (or a local server you started) is the host. On Windows you
      can add a WSL environment so Linux CLIs run in the distro. SSH hosts are
      extra backends, not pairing. A phone or another browser pairs to that
      host; it does not run the agents itself. Follow{" "}
      <a href="/docs/remote">Remote access</a> to create a pairing link and
      connect from{" "}
      <a href="/download">iOS or Android</a>. Desktop backends:{" "}
      <a href="/docs/desktop">Desktop &amp; environments</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="source">
    <h2>Run from source</h2>
    <p>
      Contributors and people who want a custom build should start at{" "}
      <a href="/docs/development">Local development</a>. The short path:
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code">
      <div class="docs-code-head"><span>From source</span><span>Shell</span></div>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Syphon1205/Modesto.git
cd Modesto
bun install
bun run modesto:dev</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <div class="docs-card-grid">
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span>First thread</span>
        <h3>Quickstart</h3>
        <p>Send a chat (no folder required), then add a project when you need a diff.</p>
        <a href="/docs/get-started">Open quickstart →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span>Safety</span>
        <h3>Permission modes</h3>
        <p>Choose when the agent asks before it edits or runs commands.</p>
        <a href="/docs/permissions">Read permissions →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="docs-card">
        <span>Git</span>
        <h3>Source control</h3>
        <p>Worktrees, commits, and opening a pull request from the thread.</p>
        <a href="/docs/source-control">Read source control →</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
